---
// Download and first run, from the README's Install, Quick start and Requirements sections (the default
// keys were checked against ink-ffi's voice.rs, the Windows minimum against windows/Inkwell.csproj, the
// model totals against ink-engines' rows). The file links are the releases' real assets (see
// ../lib/release.ts). The Mac build is signed and notarised; the Windows build is not signed yet, and
// its steps and Microsoft's terms follow windows/HOMEPAGE-INSTALL.md (keep the terms sentence in step
// with the release notes, the installer's splash and the app's terms step).
import { BUILD_FROM_SOURCE_URL, RELEASES_URL, SHARED_VERSION } from '../lib/constants';
import { DOWNLOADS } from '../lib/release';
---

<section class="install wrap" id="download" aria-labelledby="download-title">
  <div class="section-head">
    <h2 id="download-title">Download Inkwell{SHARED_VERSION && ` ${SHARED_VERSION}`}.</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      Free, with no account and no email address asked for. Every build is also on the
      <a href={RELEASES_URL}>GitHub releases page</a>, and you can <a href={BUILD_FROM_SOURCE_URL}>build it from
      source</a>. Linux and Intel Macs aren’t supported: Inkwell 0.2 stays the last version for them, on the
      same page. Windows on ARM64 isn’t supported by 1.0 either: a build for it is planned for 1.0.1.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="platforms">
    <div class="platform" data-platform="macOS">
      <div class="platform-head">
        <h3>macOS</h3>
        <p class="detected-note" hidden>This computer</p>
        <p>Apple silicon, on macOS 26 or later.</p>
        <ul class="files">
          <li><a href={DOWNLOADS.macOS.url} translate="no">{DOWNLOADS.macOS.name}</a> <span>{DOWNLOADS.macOS.size}</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="platform-body">
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            Open the disk image, drag Inkwell to Applications and open it. It is signed with a Developer ID and
            notarised by Apple, so there is no warning to click past.
          </li>
          <li>Allow Microphone access when asked.</li>
          <li>
            Allow Accessibility access in System Settings, under Privacy &amp; Security. Inkwell needs it to hear
            the dictation key and to type the text; until you grant it, dictation stays off and says why.
          </li>
          <li>For meetings, allow System Audio Recording when asked: it is how Inkwell hears the other side of a call.</li>
          <li>Calendars is optional: it lets Inkwell show your next meeting and name each meeting and who was in it.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="platform-note">
          Secure Input (a password field, some terminals) blocks typing into that app: nothing is typed, and
          Inkwell says so.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="platform" data-platform="Windows" id="windows-install">
      <div class="platform-head">
        <h3>Windows</h3>
        <p class="detected-note" hidden>This computer</p>
        <p>Windows 11 24H2 or later, on x64. It installs for your user account alone: no administrator, nothing system-wide.</p>
        <ul class="files">
          <li><a href={DOWNLOADS.windows.url} translate="no">{DOWNLOADS.windows.name}</a> <span>{DOWNLOADS.windows.size}</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="platform-body">
        {/* The README's Install section and the hero link here. */}
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            Download the installer. Your browser may say the file isn’t commonly downloaded: in Edge, open the
            download’s “…” menu, choose “Keep”, then “Show more” and “Keep anyway”; in Chrome, choose “Keep”.
          </li>
          <li>
            Check the download (recommended). The installer is not code signed yet, so this is how you know the
            file is the one we published: in PowerShell, <code>Get-FileHash</code> followed by the file’s path
            prints its SHA-256, which must match the one in the release notes. If it does not, delete the file
            and do not run it.
          </li>
          <li>
            Run it. SmartScreen says “Windows protected your PC”, with an unknown publisher: click “More info”,
            then “Run anyway”. Inkwell installs in a few seconds, with the Visual C++ runtime its speech engines
            need beside it, and opens.
          </li>
          <li>
            On its first run, Inkwell shows Microsoft’s terms for the runtimes it includes (below): agree to
            start, or quit.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="platform-note">
          If Smart App Control blocked it, there is no “Run anyway”: Smart App Control does not run apps that
          are not signed. The signed build is on its way; we would rather you wait for it than turn off a
          protection you chose to keep.
        </p>
        <p class="platform-note">
          Inkwell includes the runtime of Microsoft’s Windows App SDK, the Windows SDK’s .NET projection and
          the Visual C++ runtime, which Microsoft licenses separately under the Microsoft Software License
          Terms (shown in full in Inkwell’s Settings &gt; About). By installing or using Inkwell on Windows,
          you agree to those terms for those components.
        </p>
        <p class="platform-note">
          Updates come when you ask for them, in Settings &gt; About &gt; Check Now, from this project’s
          releases, each checked against its size and SHA-256. Uninstalling, in Settings &gt; Apps, leaves your
          library in <span translate="no">%LOCALAPPDATA%\Inkwell</span>. Inkwell 0.2 stays installed beside
          1.0: import its history before you uninstall it with its application data.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="install-after">
    <div>
      <h3>First run</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          Finish the onboarding. The installer holds no speech models: its Models step lists each with its
          licence, size and source, about 3.1&nbsp;GB in all on the Mac and 3.3&nbsp;GB on Windows, and nothing
          downloads until you press Download. You can go on while they download, or get them later in
          Settings.
        </li>
        <li>
          If Inkwell 0.2 is on this computer, onboarding offers to import its dictation history, dictionary,
          snippets and modes. 0.2’s own copy is only read, never changed.
        </li>
        <li>
          The dictation key starts as <kbd>Fn</kbd> on the Mac and right <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> on Windows. Change it in
          Settings.
        </li>
        <li>Hold the key, speak, let go. The text is typed where your cursor is.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Requirements</h3>
      <dl class="reqs">
        <div><dt>System</dt><dd>A Mac with Apple silicon on macOS 26 or later, or a PC with Windows 11 24H2 or later on x64</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Disk</dt><dd>About 3.1&nbsp;GB for the models on the Mac, 3.3&nbsp;GB on Windows, plus the meetings you keep</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Microphone</dt><dd>Any</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
